<p>The confusion on the strength is understandable, though- the falloff on magnetic field strength is pretty steep: a refrigerator magnet is 5&#160;mT, a neodynium magnet is 1.25&#160;T, 16&#160;T can levitate a frog, Sandia National Laboritory's <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Z_Pulsed_Power_Facility">Z-machine</a><sup class="footnoteref"><a id="footnoteref-41824-1" href="javascript:;" class="footnoteref" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnote-41824-1')">1</a></sup> has a typical working load between ~10-100&#160;T. They've gotten it up to a 1&#160;kT pulse at least once, but they had to fix it afterward.</p>
