					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/decibelles" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2005044); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2005044&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1605861493" alt="Decibelles" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2005044)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/decibelles" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2005044); return false;" >Decibelles</a></span> <span class="odate time_1500994818 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">25 Jul 2017 15:00</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3557620">
									

<p>There seems to be this fairly recent trend of characterizing AWCY? as evil art bastards that do evil things for art and to make statements with art. If that's where the site wants to take this then sure, but I think it's distressing considering these are the same guys who were really well-portrayed as&#8230; well, such a disparate group will never have one single unifying characteristic, but I thought for a time they were pretentious, in over their heads, but well-meaning ultimately. That's what drew me to them and made me adore them. Even Yoric's original tale that spawned them and the similar stuff created in that vein had a sense of do-gooder attitude even if they engaged in artistic terrorism (so to speak). This is&#8230; probably one of the least AWCY?-AWCY? articles I've seen in such a long time, probably since <a href="/scp-1800">SCP-1800</a>, a skip I loathe oh so much.</p>
<p>This doesn't quite reach those levels but I still am not a fan. I've been trying to think of what bothered me so much about this since I read it last night and here's what I got. I'm putting on my SJW hat for this.</p>
<p>SCP-3126 is an African-American woman, sold off as property by a white woman to a rich (presumably white from context, still fucked up either way) famous person.</p>
<p>Do you see the issue here?</p>
<p>If you were writing this as a wider commentary on its own about how deeply-rooted the issue of racism is even in art communities and among the elite, that such a power imbalance would let this sort of thing happen and how it does happen in the world but specifically (in this case) the Western world, then this wouldn't have left such a bad taste in my mouth. But the most you ever do with this is <em>hint</em> at the idea that <em>maybe</em> this is a theme that you have left unexplored, and this is assuming you were even conscious of what the problematic nature of this whole thing. If this was your overall objective, you failed because the best-case scenario is that you only ever vaguely allude to it. The worst-case scenario being that you didn't even realize you had these themes in the writing. But judging from your comments here and everyone else's, I think the latter is extremely likely.</p>
<p>There's also the fact that you've written in an explicit lesbian couple for this article, a real rarity on the entire site, and it displays a serious power imbalance between a white woman and an African-American woman, as she continues to have her power stripped away from her culminating in being sold off for profit to others. It gives this sort of weird, mid-1900s vibe to the whole thing with how these lesbians are being portrayed, and it bothers me.</p>
<p>And the reason why I suspect all these characters who are not our skip are white is because that is the typical &quot;default&quot; for characters when their ethnicity and race is not specified, and I can not conceive of anyone who isn't white being okay with <em>selling someone into slavery</em>, and if you did intend for this to happen to examine the ramifications and impact of it, and get across a meta-artistic message, <em>you don't show this in your article</em>. The most your main character, 3126, says about her old girlfriend in terms of how strong her emotion gets is that she just &quot;wanted to be cool&quot; (this style of referencing AWCY? got real old back in 2012, let alone 2017), and that &quot;everything had to be a statement&quot;.</p>
<p>And what is AWCY?'s statement here? Because I read this article through so many times just to write this comment and try and word it as best as I can, and the closest statement that I can find is &quot;art people are bastards&quot;, because I can't see any other reason provided by you in the article for why someone from this group would sell her girlfriend into slavery. I can't stress this enough; you don't even try and explore that specific thing in here. It just feels so&#8230; very not-AWCY?, and I'd have issues with this even if it weren't AWCY?, but specifically invoking them here makes this so much weaker than it is. It actively makes your message overall <em>worse</em>. Quoting someone from above:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Does being an anartist turn you into a bastard person, or do bastard people become anartists?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>There's better ways to do that than invoke AWCY?'s name again for a tired article about them (which reminds me a lot about the heavy backlash they got when they got popular), and there's certainly more impactful and more stronger ways to do so than &quot;African-American woman gets turned into art and sold into slavery&quot;. Because it would take extremely strong, smart, and thoughtful writing to turn that into a working article, though even trying for that would have been more respectful in my eyes than the product here.</p>
<p>Downvote.</p>

							</div>

								
					
