<blockquote>
<p>Reading this much between the lines, in my opinion, could lead people to actually AVOID being more diverse when writing.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>If one is going to create art for others to enjoy and take in, it's only fair that I should read as much as I want into the article as I can in order to critique it and understand it. Especially for an AWCY? article. I feel that AWCY? deserve the most in terms of actual in-depth art critique and analysis, since they are a heavily based in actually creating art, giving out a message with their art, and make their art loud and publicly enough to warrant people looking at them and absorbing their message. It's only fair that I take all AWCY? articles the same way. This is why they are, hands-down, my favorite group on the entire site. It'd be an injustice to go light here just because I'm &quot;reading this much between the lines&quot;. Plus, there's no actual correlation between giving long, thoughtful critique and people seeing this critique and deciding to not be diverse in their writing. There's not enough data to back up your assertion.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>it seems purely incidental - not only does it seem like the author had no interest going in such a direction</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is why it's bad. If I write an article about an art piece, and throw in some satellite elements that are incredibly glaring, I've distracted from my piece and drawn attention to it. If I write a piece about an artist who made an anomalous painting on the statement of money, and oh, she was a trans woman who got brutally murdered for her art, I'd get comments from people who know that trans women get murdered brutally as hate crimes.</p>
<p>Yes, I can sympathize with 3126 in the sense that she was stuck in a shitty relationship and eventually became the product of art assholes who exist purely to be assholes to other people (my major problem with the characterization of AWCY? here; see my post above). Yes, she was tricked by a selfish lover. But it's also tinted in such a way with everything else surrounding it that I feel justified in calling it out. It's not even the specific problem of someone being turned into a statue and then sold to someone else that specifically bothers me. It was the way it was handled here that bothers me.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Everybody can be an asshole.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Yes, that is true. This is true in that everyone is an asshole. It is also true in the sense that any ethnic group or race can and will sell others in slavery. <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_slavery">This is a real interesting summary of that to read, and various citations lead to some facts I honestly didn't know about before.</a> But in 1990s America? I have to examine that in the context of the story. This isn't Django Unchained.</p>

