<blockquote>
<p>Plus, there's no actual correlation between giving long, thoughtful critique and people seeing this critique and deciding to not be diverse in their writing. There's not enough data to back up your assertion.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Expressed myself incorrectly there. One is, indeed, free to examine a piece as thoroughly as one wants. I am merely concerned that, by citing this as a reason to downvote the article, other authors would simply avoid the subject entirely instead of bothering with the possibility of being intensive.</p>
<p>I don't think there's conciliation to be found on this point. It stems from the fact you see the article as flawed in its ethos, and I don't concur.</p>
<p>Again, I agree with all points raised about AWCY?. It's a complex GOI, and they are so much more than a silly catch-phrase.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>she was a trans woman who got brutally murdered for her art, I'd get comments from people who know that trans women get murdered brutally as hate crimes.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is probably where our line of thinking differs the most. I did understand immediately what you meant in the first comment: If you don't understand physics, don't throw it in the middle of your article - this is true for any subject. It's just that, in 3126's case, I see nothing remotely comparable to your example.</p>
<p>There are two characters, one of which is a shithead and they happen to be from different ethnic groups. Looking further than that frankly seems like a stretch to me, which is probably another case of incompatibility between our interpretation of the piece.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>But in 1990s America? I have to examine that in the context of the story. This isn't Django Unchained.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Quite fair. Still, as the article you linked very thoroughly documents, the scenario described is far from impossible.</p>
<p>I think you are completely justified in your vote - the article, again, does not depict AWCY? well and it seems to indeed offend you. I, personally, however, can't fault the author for interlacing what could be seen as a slavery allegory in the middle of a simple story about anomalies and abusive relationships, if you squint hard enough.</p>

