				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/yarrun" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2118220); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2118220&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1605861493" alt="Yarrun" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2118220)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/yarrun" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2118220); return false;" >Yarrun</a></span> <span class="odate time_1503289613 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">21 Aug 2017 04:26</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3575012">
									

<p>Part of what drove me to attempt an AWCY?-related SCP was to, in fact, do something different. There's a short story in the AWCY? tag where The Critic (well, A Critic, knowing the fragmented nature of AWCY) goes on a rant about how so much of AWCY?'s output is focused around inflicting suffering. More about harming people than actually being artful. That's why the actual art piece explores its concept (i.e. expanding the definition of what can be called a statue) in a way that isn't inherently harmful&#8212;the problem only arises because they didn't understand the tools they were using. Additionally, Gloria is the victim of actions perpetrated by a member of AWCY?, yes, but the third interview and a bit of the second imply that she's also a believer in the principles under AWCY? herself (if an estranged one for obvious reasons). She therefore serves as a counterpoint to Bellamy's more questionable actions. Horrible things are done, but there is also a legitimate desire to explore artistic ideas, and I feel that's enough to separate it from the usual 'AWCY? are just trolls hiding behind art' stereotypes.</p>
<p>I will admit that Bellamy ended up being a bit more of an asshole than I intended. Earlier versions had Bellamy either get talked into the sale, or the sale happening behind her back, or her disappearing for ambiguous reasons without leaving instructions on how to unstatue Gloria. And given the genders and orientations in play, that led to some implications that I didn't intend. I also could have fleshed out the second and third interviews a bit, and made some of the more redeeming elements more explicit but I was already nervous about the interviews having too much exposition and I didn't want to overdo it. That said, while the auction was a late-game addition and I didn't initially realize the racial implications, I intentionally left it in after the realization. One of the themes running through the article is objectification, with Gloria literally turned into an object for the sake of art and eventually ending up with the SCP, which objectifies everything it contains by default (and that, in turn, flows into the general objectification of black Americans by white Americans as a whole and white artists and entertainers in particular, but I digress). I, <span style="text-decoration: underline;">as a Black man in America</span>, thought that the connection to American slavery auctions dovetailed rather nicely.</p>
<p>I could always go back in and give Bellamy a bit more credit in the second interview, I guess. Not sure what the etiquette is for rewriting a work after it's already been posted, as opposed to editing out mistakes.</p>

							</div>

								
