			<div class="content" id="post-content-3604855">
									

<p>You're the original author: my understanding is that you can totally rewrite or even delete this at your leisure.</p>
<p>I advise focusing your efforts on improving the science and reducing cringe, though.</p>
<p>As others pointed out, 1 Tesla is <em>huge</em>. Even a few milli-Tesla is enough to move paperclips. For lesser magnetic fields, I'd be more worried about the current flowing through your average power cable than anything a PC produces (unless you're physically touching the hard drive).</p>
<p>The link to SCP-1057 looks like you're trying to ride on another article's popularity &#8212; if you're really set on forcing a reference to another SCP in, at least choose something that could use more exposure. Also, the way you had her recognize the &quot;S.C.P.&quot; initials basically put a spotlight on an ugly plothole.</p>
<p>I wouldn't worry about &quot;fixing&quot; the &quot;racial issues&quot;. For an SCP, having an accidental extra layer of disturbing implications is actually a good thing. Leaving the deeper social 'power imbalance' unexplored is fine, since it's clear you're not depicting this as a normal or acceptable state of affairs.</p>

							</div>

								
					
