					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/nnelg" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1778359); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1778359&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1605861493" alt="Nnelg" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1778359)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/nnelg" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1778359); return false;" >Nnelg</a></span> <span class="odate time_1506796368 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">30 Sep 2017 18:32</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3604908">
									

<blockquote>
<p>SCP-3126 is an African-American woman, sold off as property by a white woman to a rich (presumably white from context, still fucked up either way) famous person.</p>
<p>Do you see the issue here?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Oh. Wow, yeah. That flew right over my head.</p>
<p>An SCP with such subtle horror deserves better than my downvote. Upgrading to neutral.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>If you were writing this as a wider commentary on its own about how deeply-rooted the issue of racism is even in art communities and among the elite, that such a power imbalance would let this sort of thing happen and how it does happen in the world but specifically (in this case) the Western world, then this wouldn't have left such a bad taste in my mouth.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Do you need a full diatribe regarding the horrors of war in every work featuring a veteran with implied PTSD? What about the socio-economic causes of poverty and crime in superhero comics?</p>
<p>Racism is horrible, but it's no different from any other horrible thing. Treating it as taboo is counterproductive.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I can not conceive of anyone who isn't white being okay with <em>selling someone into slavery</em></p>
</blockquote>
<p>No offense, but that's at least as racist as assuming a rapist is black.</p>
<p>If you want to add the qualifier &quot;in 1900s America&quot;, I can add the qualifier &quot;in downtown Chicago&quot;. It's still a form of racial profiling <em>at best.</em></p>
<blockquote>
<p>There's also the fact that you've written in an explicit lesbian couple for this article, a real rarity on the entire site, and it displays a serious power imbalance between a white woman and an African-American woman, as she continues to have her power stripped away from her culminating in being sold off for profit to others. It gives this sort of weird, mid-1900s vibe to the whole thing with how these lesbians are being portrayed, and it bothers me.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>In the 2010's, though, the general understanding is that homosexual relationships are basically the same as heterosexual ones. It thus follows that they have the same potential for becoming abusive.</p>
<p>Again, a taboo is not in any way helpful. True equality means homosexuals are subject to the same spectrum of positive and negative depictions as heterosexuals.</p>
<hr />
<p>I will say though, that your issues with the AWCY? portrayal are entirely valid.</p>

